"""## **Creating Derived columns**

## **Derived a new column as **Available_Stock** **
The actual usable stock available for fulfilling new orders. Subtracts all reserved, committed, and damaged quantities from on-hand stock.
 this one is mainly used for Used to trigger replenishment decisions and prevent overselling
"""

df1 = df.copy()

df1

"""## **INVENTORY ANALYSIS**

### The inventory turnover analysis classifies products into slow, medium, fast, and very fast-moving categories. A portion of items fall under slow-moving, indicating potential overstocking and capital lock-in. Meanwhile, very fast-moving items highlight high demand and possible stockout risks, suggesting the need for improved replenishment strategies.
"""

df['Available_Stock'] = (
    df['Quantity_On_Hand']
    - df['Quantity_Reserved']
    - df['Quantity_Committed']
    - df['Damaged_Qty']
)
df['Available_Stock']

# Stock Availability Status
df['New_Stock_Status'] = df['Available_Stock'].apply(
    lambda x: 'Out of Stock' if x <= 0 else 'Low' if x < 50 else 'In Stock'
)

"""### I categorized inventory into Critical, Low, Healthy, and Excess based on stock coverage (Days of Inventory). This classification helps quickly identify SKUs at risk of stockouts as well as overstock situations, enabling better inventory planning and optimization of working capital."""

import seaborn as sns
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.figure(figsize=(7,5))
sns.countplot(x="New_Stock_Status", data=df)

plt.title("Stock Availability Status Distribution")
plt.xlabel("Stock Status")
plt.ylabel("Number of Products")

plt.show()

# Stock coverage
df["Days_of_Inventory"] = (
    df["Quantity_On_Hand"] /
    df["Avg_Daily_Sales"].replace(0, np.nan)
)

df["Days_of_Inventory"] = df["Days_of_Inventory"].round(2)

conditions = [
    df["Days_of_Inventory"] <= 10,
    df["Days_of_Inventory"].between(10, 30),
    df["Days_of_Inventory"].between(30, 60),
    df["Days_of_Inventory"] > 60
]

choices = ["Critical", "Low", "Healthy", "Excess"]

df["Stock_Status"] = np.select(conditions, choices, default="Unknown")

plt.figure(figsize=(7,5))
sns.countplot(x="Stock_Status", data=df)

plt.title("Stock Status Distribution (Based on Days of Inventory)")
plt.xlabel("Stock Status")
plt.ylabel("Number of Products")

plt.show()

"""### Coverage Gap was created to quantify the exact number of days a product will experience stockout before new inventory arrives. Unlike binary stockout indicators, it provides the severity of shortage risk, enabling prioritization of urgent replenishment decisions.

If Lead Time is bigger → GAP exists

If Inventory is bigger → No GAP
"""

# Inventory Coverage Gap
df["Coverage_Gap"] = (
    df["Lead_Time_Days"] - df["Days_of_Inventory"]
).clip(lower=0)

import seaborn as sns
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))
sns.histplot(df["Coverage_Gap"], bins=30, kde=True)

plt.title("Distribution of Inventory Coverage Gap")
plt.xlabel("Coverage Gap (Days)")
plt.ylabel("Number of Products")

plt.show()

"""Inventory Coverage Gap measures the difference between supplier lead time and available inventory days. A positive gap indicates potential stockouts before replenishment arrives. This helps identify high-risk items and improve procurement planning.

"""

# Inventory Turnover Proxy
df["Inventory_Turnover"] = df["Avg_Daily_Sales"] * 365 / df["Quantity_On_Hand"]

def turnover_label(x):
    if pd.isna(x):
        return "No Stock"
    elif x >= 50:
        return "Very Fast Moving"
    elif x >= 20:
        return "Fast Moving"
    elif x >= 5:
        return "Medium Moving"
    else:
        return "Slow Moving"
df["Turnover_Category"] = df["Inventory_Turnover"].apply(turnover_label)

df[["Inventory_Turnover", "Turnover_Category"]]

df.groupby("Category")["Quantity_On_Hand"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)

df.pivot_table(values="Quantity_On_Hand",
               index="Warehouse_ID",
               columns="Category",
               aggfunc="sum")

df["Turnover_Category"] = df["Inventory_Turnover"].apply(turnover_label)

turnover_counts = df["Turnover_Category"].value_counts()

plt.figure()
turnover_counts.plot(kind='bar')
plt.title("Inventory Turnover Categories")
plt.xlabel("Turnover Category")
plt.ylabel("Number of Products")
plt.xticks(rotation=30)
plt.show()

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Create cross table
cross_analysis = pd.crosstab(df['Stock_Status'], df['Turnover_Category'])

# Reorder columns (optional for clarity)
cross_analysis = cross_analysis[['Fast Moving', 'Medium Moving', 'Very Fast Moving']]

# Plot grouped bar chart
ax = cross_analysis.plot(kind='bar')

plt.title("Stock Level vs Movement Type")
plt.xlabel("Stock Status")
plt.ylabel("Number of Products")
plt.xticks(rotation=0)
plt.legend(title="Movement Type")

# Add value labels on bars
for container in ax.containers:
    ax.bar_label(container)

plt.show()

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.figure()
plt.imshow(cross_analysis)

plt.title("Stock vs Movement Heatmap")
plt.xlabel("Movement Type")
plt.ylabel("Stock Status")

plt.xticks(range(len(cross_analysis.columns)), cross_analysis.columns)
plt.yticks(range(len(cross_analysis.index)), cross_analysis.index)

# Add values inside cells
for i in range(len(cross_analysis.index)):
    for j in range(len(cross_analysis.columns)):
        plt.text(j, i, cross_analysis.iloc[i, j],
                 ha='center', va='center')

plt.colorbar()
